2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: F

The circumflex in French is an etymological / historical orthographic marker. In its documented history it is used:

* B to keep homophones distinct, e.g. sur / sûr, du / dû, la / là;
* D to retain the historical trace of Old French long vowels, the long vowel being the reflex of Latin length / diphthongisation;
* G to mark a vowel that is the monophthongal outcome of an earlier diphthong, e.g. âme < anima, maître < magister;
* H to mark a consonant, most often a sibilant /s/, that has been lost in the spoken and written form, e.g. hôte < hoste, forêt < forest, etc.;
* A is the phonetic correlate of the historical length / monophthongisation, e.g. ê ~ /ɛ/ vs é ~ /e/, â ~ /ɑ/, ô ~ /o/; the quality contrast is a reflex of the historical marking.
* I is the same hiatus / diphthongal reduction class as G.

C and D are the same historical length reflex in Latin-derived material, the circumflex being the graphic reflex of Latin long ō > ô, etc.

Prestige / stylisation is not a codified orthographic function of the accent. The circumflex is retained as an etymological / disambiguating device and it is not introduced in the system in order to make a word look more learned or elegant. The “fancy” effect is a modern sociolinguistic perception, not an attested orthographic function.

9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. To indicate contrastive distinctions between closed and open vowel sounds.

The French accent circonflexe is an orthographic / etymological marker, not a synchronic quality marker. In its attested history it is used

* B to keep homophones distinct, e.g. sur /syʁ/ ‘on’ vs sûr /syʁ/ ‘sure’, mur /myʁ/ ‘wall’ vs mûr /myʁ/ ‘ripe’, du /dy/ vs dû /dy/;
* H/G to record the loss of a post-vocalic consonant, most characteristically an s, and the consequent historical lengthening/monophthongisation that followed, e.g. forêt < forest, hôte < hospite, bête < bestia, etc.;
* I / G to record the monophthongisation of an earlier diphthong and, in the same formal context, the resolution of a former hiatus created by that loss;
* D in a diachronic sense to mark the length that resulted from consonantal loss, and therefore the classic description of the accent as a length / loss marker.

A synchronic closed vs open vowel contrast is not what the circumflex is doing in French. The open /ɛ/ vs closed /e/ and open /ɔ/ vs closed /o/ oppositions are normally signalled by the acute/grave system and by syllable structure, not by the circumflex as a contrastive feature. The value of â, ê, î, ô, û is essentially fixed in modern pronunciation and the accent is not used to create a minimal open/closed pair in the present synchronic system.

